Kloak - Complete Technical Documentation
1. Project Overview and Core Philosophy
What is Kloak?
Kloak is a production-ready, privacy-first Discord clone built as a real-time communication platform. The fundamental philosophy that distinguishes Kloak from other chat platforms is its unwavering commitment to user privacy. The core principle is: "Privacy is not a feature. It's the architecture."

Privacy-First Approach
Unlike traditional platforms that require email addresses, phone numbers, or passwords, Kloak uses a revolutionary authentication system based entirely on cryptographically generated secret keys. Users receive a 64-character hexadecimal key upon account creation that serves as their sole means of authentication. This key is:

Generated client-side using crypto.getRandomValues()
Hashed with SHA-256 before being stored in the database
Never transmitted in plain form after the initial display
Unrecoverable if lost (by design)
This approach eliminates the need for personal identifying information, making it impossible to track users across platforms or recover accounts through social engineering.

2. Technology Stack
Frontend Architecture
The frontend is built on a modern React-based stack:

React 18+ with TypeScript for type-safe component development
Vite as the build tool for fast development and optimized production builds
React Router v6 for client-side routing with nested route support
Tailwind CSS for utility-first styling with a neutral color palette
shadcn/ui components built on Radix UI primitives for accessible UI elements
Framer Motion for animations and transitions
Lucide React for consistent iconography
State Management
Zustand is used for global state management due to its simplicity and performance. The application maintains several interconnected stores:

authStore: User authentication, session management, and presence status
serverStore: Servers, channels, categories, members, roles, and permissions
messageStore: Channel messages with per-channel caching
dmStore: Direct message conversations and messages
friendStore: Friendships, pending requests, and blocked users
uiStore: Theme, modals, sidebar states, and preferences
notificationStore: User notifications
voiceStore: Voice channel presence and connection state
offlineQueueStore: Queued operations for offline support
syncStore: Connection and synchronization status
Backend Infrastructure
The backend is powered by Supabase:

PostgreSQL database with comprehensive schema
Row Level Security (RLS) policies (set to permissive due to custom auth)
SECURITY DEFINER RPC functions for secure mutations
Supabase Realtime for instant message delivery, reactions, and presence
Supabase Storage for avatars, server icons, and attachments
Edge Functions for server-side operations (link previews, Discord template import)
Desktop Application
Kloak also supports a native desktop experience through Tauri, providing:

Native window management
System tray integration
Desktop notifications
Cross-platform support (Windows, macOS, Linux)
3. Authentication System
Secret Key Generation
When a user creates an account, the following process occurs:

User enters a username (3-20 characters, alphanumeric + underscores)
Username availability is checked via check_username_available RPC
A 64-character hex secret key is generated using crypto.getRandomValues()
The key is hashed using SHA-256 (with a pure JS fallback for non-HTTPS contexts)
The register_user RPC creates the user record with the hashed key
The plain key is displayed ONCE to the user with strong warnings to save it
Login Process
Authentication works by:

User pastes their 64-character secret key
Key format is validated (must be 64 hex characters)
Key is hashed client-side using SHA-256
login_user RPC searches for a matching key_hash in the database
If found, user data is returned and session is established
The plain key is stored in localStorage (persisted via Zustand's persist middleware)
Session Persistence
The auth store uses Zustand's persist middleware to maintain sessions:

secretKey is stored encrypted in localStorage under kloak-auth
manualStatus (for DND/Invisible) is also persisted
On app load, checkAuth() validates the stored key against the database
Rate Limiting
Security measures include:

Account creation: 3 attempts per 5 minutes
Login attempts: 5 per minute
Rate limiters use sliding window algorithm with automatic cleanup
4. Database Architecture
Core Tables
Users Table

id (UUID, primary key)
username (unique, 20 char max)
key_hash (64 char, SHA-256 hash of secret key)
display_name, avatar_url, banner_url, banner_color
avatar_decoration, profile_effect (customization)
bio, custom_status
status (online/idle/dnd/offline)
created_at, last_seen timestamps
Servers Table

id, name, icon_url, banner_url
owner_id (references users)
invite_code (unique, 8 characters)
is_published (for Explore feature)
about (server description)
System channel settings for join/leave messages
Channels Table

id, server_id, category_id
name, topic, type (text/voice/announcement/forum/media)
position, is_nsfw, slowmode_seconds
is_read_only, is_private, ghost_mode
block_links, allowed_link_domains[]
Messages Table

id, channel_id, user_id
reply_to_id (self-reference for threading)
content, is_pinned, is_system_message
edited_at, created_at
Relationship Tables
server_members: Links users to servers with roles and metadata
channel_categories: Organizes channels into collapsible groups
message_attachments: File uploads linked to messages
reactions: Emoji reactions on messages
friendships: Bidirectional friend relationships with status
dm_conversations + dm_participants: Direct messaging
channel_reads: Unread tracking per user per channel
notifications: User notification queue
server_roles + server_member_roles: Custom role system
role_permissions + channel_permissions: Permission overrides
RPC Functions
Due to the custom authentication (no Supabase Auth), RLS policies are permissive and sensitive operations use SECURITY DEFINER RPC functions:

register_user, login_user, check_username_available
create_server_with_owner, join_server_by_invite, leave_server
send_message, edit_message, delete_message
get_user_servers, get_server_channels, get_server_members
get_all_channel_unread_counts (batch optimized)
Friend request RPCs: send_friend_request, accept_friend_request, etc.
5. State Management Architecture
Store Design Patterns
Each Zustand store follows consistent patterns:

State Shape: Typed interface defining all state properties
Actions: Methods that modify state or trigger side effects
Selectors: Computed values derived from state
Persistence: Optional localStorage persistence for session data
Data Flow
User Action → Store Action → Supabase RPC → Database
                                  ↓
                            Realtime Subscription
                                  ↓
                         Store Update → UI Re-render
Caching Strategy
The messageStore implements intelligent caching:

Messages are cached per channel with 5-minute TTL
Pagination is supported through before parameter
Force refresh available for visibility changes
Cache invalidation on channel switch
Cross-Store Communication
Stores can access each other through direct imports:

useAuthStore.getState() for user data in any context
useFriendStore.getState().visibilityBlockedUserIds for filtering blocked users
6. Real-time System
Supabase Realtime Configuration
Tables are configured with REPLICA IDENTITY FULL and included in the supabase_realtime publication:

messages, reactions, channels, categories
direct_messages, dm_reactions
notifications, friendships
server_members, users
Subscription Hooks
Real-time subscriptions are managed at the AppLayout level for stability:

useRealtimeMessages: Subscribes to message INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE for the current channel

User data cached with 60-second TTL to prevent redundant fetches
Blocked users filtered from incoming messages
Handles replies by looking up existing messages
useRealtimeReactions: Tracks reaction changes and triggers re-grouping

useRealtimeDMs: Global subscription for direct messages across all conversations

useRealtimeNotifications: Listens for new notifications to update badges

useRealtimeFriendships: Tracks friend request changes

Typing Indicators
Implemented using Supabase Broadcast (ephemeral, not persisted):

startTyping() broadcasts typing event, debounced to every 2 seconds
stopTyping() broadcasts stop event after 2 seconds of inactivity
Recipients display typing users with animated dots
Connection Recovery
The system handles connection drops gracefully:

Visibility change events trigger subscription health checks
Failed subscriptions retry with exponential backoff (max 2 retries)
Reconnection fetches missed messages
