All data that can change — messages, edits, deletions, user presence, role changes, channel updates, server settings, member lists, permissions — must use Supabase Realtime subscriptions to reflect changes instantly in the UI. Never build features that require the user to refresh the page or navigate away and back to see updated data.
Implementation pattern:

Use supabase.channel() with .on('postgres_changes', ...) to subscribe to INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE events on relevant tables.
Update Zustand store state directly from realtime event payloads so all subscribed components re-render automatically.
Subscriptions should be established when a component mounts or a context is entered (e.g., joining a server/channel) and cleaned up on unmount/exit.
Optimistic UI updates are encouraged for user-initiated actions (send message, edit, delete), but the realtime subscription must still handle the confirmed server event to ensure consistency across all connected clients.
Never rely solely on refetching data after a mutation. The mutation confirms success; the realtime subscription propagates the change to all clients including the sender.

This applies to all features including but not limited to:

Messages (new, edited, deleted)
Channel creation, editing, deletion
Server settings and metadata changes
Member joins, leaves, role assignments
User profile updates and presence/status
Permission and role changes
Typing indicators
File uploads and attachments
Reactions and read receipts

If a feature cannot use Supabase Realtime (e.g., external service data), implement polling with a reasonable interval and document why realtime isn't possible.
